INT. CAR WASH - TUNNEL - NIGHT

Blue foam. Spinning brushes. A conveyor drags a black sedan through the dark. The attendant booth glows at the end.

RAYMUNDO "RAY" OYELOWO, 40s, uniform, towel on shoulder, waits at the exit. At his side, TANISHA BEAUMONT, 20s, trainee, holds a squeegee like a baton.

A second man leans on the booth wall: MARCUS DELACROIX-WU, 50s, leather jacket, thumb in his belt.

The sedan emerges, dripping.

RAY
Marcus.

MARCUS
Ray.

RAY
Car's clean.

MARCUS
Is it?

Tanisha wipes the windshield, too fast.

TANISHA
Spotless, sir.

MARCUS
Easy, kid. You'll rub off the paint.

She stops. He opens the trunk. Looks inside. Closes it.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Ray. Come here.

Ray steps close. Marcus puts a hand on his shoulder. Squeezes. Smiles.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Good work.

He pulls three twenties from his jacket. Tucks them into Ray's breast pocket. Pats it.

TANISHA
(whispering to Ray)
What was in the trunk?

RAY
Nothing.

MARCUS
(already in the car)
Exactly.

The sedan rolls out into the rain. Tanisha looks at the three twenties poking from Ray's pocket. Ray's hand trembles once. Then he folds the bills away and turns on the hose.
